1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:03,836
What if Sarawak had never left Japanese rule?

2
00:00:03,886 --> 00:00:08,846
In 1945, the Japanese occupation of Sarawak ended.

3
00:00:08,886 --> 00:00:10,992
Now imagine it never did.

4
00:00:11,032 --> 00:00:14,203
This is one possible alternate history.

5
00:00:16,693 --> 00:00:19,213
For about a hundred years, Sarawak was ruled

6
00:00:19,253 --> 00:00:22,925
by the Brooke family, known as the White Rajahs.

7
00:00:22,975 --> 00:00:27,095
In December 1941, Japanese forces landed at Miri,

8
00:00:27,135 --> 00:00:29,170
and soon took Kuching.

9
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:33,106
The occupation lasted almost four years.

10
00:00:33,146 --> 00:00:34,686
Food ran short.

11
00:00:34,726 --> 00:00:36,606
Prisoners of war and civilians were held

12
00:00:36,646 --> 00:00:39,550
at Batu Lintang camp, in Kuching.

13
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:44,300
In 1945, Allied special forces parachuted into the interior

14
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:47,703
and fought alongside Dayak communities.

15
00:00:47,753 --> 00:00:48,833
That September,

16
00:00:48,873 --> 00:00:51,989
the Japanese commander in Kuching surrendered.

17
00:00:52,779 --> 00:00:55,719
Now imagine the war ends in a settlement instead,

18
00:00:55,759 --> 00:00:58,380
and Japan keeps Borneo.

19
00:00:58,430 --> 00:01:01,430
No liberation. No return of the Rajahs.

20
00:01:01,470 --> 00:01:03,089
No British colony.

21
00:01:03,739 --> 00:01:07,601
Japan might rule directly, or through local leaders.

22
00:01:07,641 --> 00:01:10,381
In real history, it gave Burma and the Philippines

23
00:01:10,421 --> 00:01:15,296
nominal independence in 1943, but kept control.

24
00:01:15,346 --> 00:01:18,816
A puppet Borneo state is one possibility.

25
00:01:19,366 --> 00:01:23,126
Oil was a key reason Japan invaded Borneo.

26
00:01:23,166 --> 00:01:23,626
Here,

27
00:01:23,666 --> 00:01:25,246
Miri's oilfields might pump for

28
00:01:25,286 --> 00:01:28,014
Japanese industry for decades.

29
00:01:28,064 --> 00:01:30,404
Schools might teach in Japanese,

30
00:01:30,444 --> 00:01:33,143
and jobs might favour those who learned it.

31
00:01:33,193 --> 00:01:36,513
Iban, Malay, Chinese, Bidayuh, Melanau

32
00:01:36,553 --> 00:01:39,743
and other languages might survive mostly at home.

33
00:01:40,373 --> 00:01:43,593
During the real occupation, communities were treated differently,

34
00:01:43,633 --> 00:01:45,849
and fear was common.

35
00:01:45,899 --> 00:01:49,199
If it lasted, resistance might continue in the interior,

36
00:01:49,239 --> 00:01:52,485
as it did in 1945.

37
00:01:52,525 --> 00:01:55,705
Or a new generation might demand independence,

38
00:01:55,745 --> 00:01:58,079
as happened across Asia.

39
00:01:58,789 --> 00:02:01,829
So where might Sarawak be by the 1970s?

40
00:02:01,869 --> 00:02:03,486
No one can know.

41
00:02:03,536 --> 00:02:06,294
Perhaps a tightly controlled territory.

42
00:02:06,334 --> 00:02:10,066
Perhaps an independent state, won through long struggle.

43
00:02:10,106 --> 00:02:13,472
Perhaps a pawn, traded between rival powers.

44
00:02:14,442 --> 00:02:18,082
What really happened: liberation in 1945,

45
00:02:18,122 --> 00:02:23,522
a British colony in 1946, and Malaysia in 1963.

46
00:02:23,572 --> 00:02:27,432
Even then, many Sarawakians opposed becoming a colony.

47
00:02:27,472 --> 00:02:29,387
Nothing was automatic.

48
00:02:29,757 --> 00:02:32,974
Which future do you think Sarawak would have had?

